// ============================================================
// COMMON CALLBACK DESIGN NOTES
// ============================================================

// HPL2 scripting is heavily callback-driven.
//
// Typical flow:
//
// player interaction
// -> callback
// -> timer
// -> entity state change
// -> sound/effect
// -> another callback
//
// Most gameplay scripting is event chaining.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
//
// GOOD PRACTICE:
//
// Use descriptive callback names:
//
// Bad:
// Func1()
// Event()
//
// Good:
// LeverPulled_MainHall()
// MonsterSpawnTimer()
// MachineSequenceFinished()
//
// Best (depicted with X and Y)
//
// X marks the name of the object we are referencing. It is useful to use it as a naming convention when we know the actual name.
// Y is then used as a second object, when two are references in the same context.
//
// LookAtMirrorFunc() - where X == mirror
// CollidePlayerwithGateCallback - where X == Player and Y == Gate
//
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------
//
// CALLBACKS ARE THE CORE OF HPL2 SCRIPTING.
//
// Most gameplay logic is built from:
// - callbacks
// - timers
// - entity states
// - chained events
//
// NOT from continuous update loops.
// EXCEPT from patch 1.5 where OnUpdate may be introduced for a FEW things
//
// ============================================================